<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seleucia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seleucia-on-Tigris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seleucia, or Seleucia-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, was a city founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around the end of the fourth century BCE. It served as one of the twin capitals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and became one of the largest cities of the Hellenistic world. It later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, as the Seleucid Empire profile notes in discussing the loss of Mesopotamia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucia.jpg" alt="Seleucia" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renato de carvalho ferreira · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ctesiphon_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
